e critique.","level":1,"path_id":"1~16","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":21,"section_id":"sec-1~16"},{"title":"Updated Solution: Give an updated selection of 128 genes based","text":"on the critique separated by commas in this format:: 1. &lt;Gene name 1&gt;, 2. &lt;Gene name 2&gt; ...Try to first focus a lot on trying very diverse genes to get a sense of which types of genes affect the research problem the most. From the observations, update your beliefs quickly and smartly and then double down on genes which you think shall be hits in order to increase the cumulative hit rate.Please do not critique/make a lot of changes if there is no need to make a change. ============================================== LLM Critic Assistant: ===================claude-1 response (2000)===================== Here are my critiques and suggestions for the next round of experiment:1. Critique: The selected genes seem very focused on mitochondrial and respiratory chain components. While these are important for interferon production, testing so many of them may limit the diversity of pathways explored. I would suggest balancing these genes with some involved in other pathways important for interferon regulation, such as NF-kB signaling, MAPK signaling, and cytosolic DNA sensing.88. ALC1 89. PARP9 90. PARP14 91. DDX60L 92. IFI16 93. cGAS 94. MB21D1 95. MB21D2 96. ABCE1 97. DHX9 98. DHX36 99. DDX1 100. DDX21 101. DDX31 102. DDX41 103. DDX42 104. DDX43 105. DDX46 106. DDX47 107. DDX48 108. DDX49 109. DDX50 110. DDX51 111. DDX52 112. DDX53 113. DDX54 114. DDX55 115. DDX56 116. DDX59 117. DDX60 118. DHX15 119. DHX30 120. DHX32 121. DHX33 122. DHX34 123. DHX35 124. DHX37 125. DHX38 126. DHX57 127. DHX8 128. DDX3X =============================================Solution: [1. MAPK1, 2. NFKB1, 3. PTPN2, 4. FOXO1, 5. CEBPA, ... in the next round","level":2,"path_id":"2.","parent_section_id":"sec-2","order_in_parent":0,"section_id":"sec-2."},{"title":"Figure 1 :","text":"1Figure1: An AI agent for closed-loop experiment design. (a) Conventional Bayesian optimization approach for experiment design involves training a machine learning model in every experimental round, scoring all perturbations and defining an acquisition function for selecting genes to perturb in the next round. (b) Overview of BioDiscoveryAgent. In every round, the agent is given a prompt that describes the research problem, its role and task as well as experimental results from previous rounds. It generates a set of genes to perturb in the next round and provides reasoning for its prediction. The agent primarily makes use of a large language model for its predictions but also has access to additional tools such as the ability to search for relevant scientific papers on the internet.","level":1,"path_id":"1~17","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":22,"section_id":"sec-1~17"},{"title":"Figure 2 :","text":"2Figure 2: Per-round performance comparison to machine learning baselines for 1-gene perturbation experiments: Each line averages the hit ratio from 10 runs, with error bars indicating 1 standard deviation. 128 genes were predicted per round over 5 rounds. For Scharenberg et al. (2023), a batch size of 32 was used due to its smaller size of 1061 perturbations.","level":1,"path_id":"1~18","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":23,"section_id":"sec-1~18"},{"title":"Figure 3 :","text":"3Figure 3: Agent accounts for both prior knowledge and observations in decision-making Three scenarios are considered: BioDiscoveryAgent (Claude 3.5 Sonnet) has access to task description and experimental observations (Prompt + Observation); only has access to experimental observations (Observation Only); only has access to the task description (Prompt Only). (a) Hit ratio at each experimental round across 30 rounds with 32 genes predicted in each. Each line corresponds to the average over 8 runs with error bars representing 1SD. (b) Jaccard similarity index between all predicted genes at 30 rounds of experimentation. Each cell corresponds to a different model run.","level":1,"path_id":"1~19","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":24,"section_id":"sec-1~19"},{"title":"Figure 5 :","text":"5Figure 5: BioDiscoveryAgent workflow with all tools over a single experimental round. Prompts and agent responses have been summarized. See Appendix G for full trace. (a) The input to the agent is the description of the problem. (b) In case of the literature search tool, the LLM first determines appropriate search terms for finding relevant scientific papers. Top ranked retrieved papers are summarized by the LLM (along with line numbers that reference directly to text in the paper) and provided as additional context to the agent when predicting the set of genes to perturb.(c) The agent predicts the set of genes to perturb in the next experiment. Response is structured containing Reflection, Research Plan and Solution. (d) In case of using the gene search tool, the LLM identifies a query gene with which to search for other genes (e)The LLM can also be prompted differently to function as a critic and analyze the predictions so far from a different perspective (f) Final output gene list after using different tools. We used Claude v1 for this analysis since it showed high absolute score as well as high performance gain through tool use for this dataset(Schmidt et al., 2022).","level":1,"path_id":"1~20","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":25,"section_id":"sec-1~20"},{"title":"Figure 6 :","text":"6Figure6: Jaccard similarity index between all predicted hits at 5 rounds of experimentation Each cell corresponds to the union of predicted hits across 10 model runs for each dataset.","level":1,"path_id":"1~21","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":26,"section_id":"sec-1~21"},{"title":"Figure 8 :","text":"8Figure 8: Agent accounts for both prior knowledge and observations in decision-making Three scenarios are considered: BioDiscoveryAgent (Claude v1) has access to task description and experimental observations (Prompt + Observation); the agent only has access to experimental observations (Observation Only); the agent only has access to the task description (Prompt Only). (a) Hit ratio at each experimental round across 30 rounds of experiments. 32 genes predicted per round. Each line corresponds to the average over 10 runs with error bars representing 1 standard deviation. (b) Jaccard similarity index between all predicted genes at 10 rounds of experimentation. Each cell corresponds to a different model run.","level":1,"path_id":"1~22","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":27,"section_id":"sec-1~22"},{"title":"Figure 9 :","text":"9Figure 9: Agent shows consistent performance for the first 30 rounds BioDiscoveryAgent (Claude 3.5 Sonnet) is tested in two different settings to observe its performance over time: hit ratio at each experimental round across 30 rounds of experiments with (a) 32 genes predicted per round and (b) 128 genes per round. Each line corresponds to the average over 5 runs with error bars representing 1 standard deviation. Scharenberg et al. (2023) is only tested with a batch size of 32 due to smaller overall size of the dataset: 1061 perturbations","level":1,"path_id":"1~23","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":28,"section_id":"sec-1~23"},{"title":"Table 7 :","text":"7Performance comparison to machine learning baselines for 1-gene perturbation experiments. Results show hit ratio for experimental round 5 averaged over 10 runs, with error intervals showing 1 standard deviation. * For Scharenberg22, a batch size of 32 was used due to its smaller pool of 1061 relevant genes. † CAR-T refers to an unpublished dataset. BDAgent stands for BioDiscoveryAgent.ModelSchmidt1 Schmidt2 CAR-T  † Scharen.  *  Carnev. Sanchez AverageAll N/E All N/E All N/E All N/E All N/E All N/E RankClaude 3 Haiku7788579888667.25GPT-3.5-Turbo9999998988898.67Claude v16556854661776.00o1-mini3333326255113.08Claude 3 Sonnet5665783432424.58Claude 3.5 Sonnet 1112231122321.75GPT-4o8877562277986.33o1-preview3444115532223.00Claude 3 Opus2211447715553.67","level":1,"path_id":"1~24","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":29,"section_id":"sec-1~24"},{"title":"Table 8 :","text":"8Rankings of models for each dataset, with ties assigned the lower rank. Bold indicates the best performance; underlined indicates the second-best performance.Tools UsedSchmidt1Schmidt2CAR-TScharen.Carnev.SanchezRandom0.037 ± 0.013 0.031 ± 0.002 0.033 ± 0.003 0.160 ± 0.028 0.036 ± 0.001 0.034 ± 0.004","level":1,"path_id":"1~25","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":30,"section_id":"sec-1~25"},{"title":"Table 10 :","text":"10Effect of tools on different LLMs. Results show hit ratio for experimental round 5 averaged over 10 runs, with error intervals showing 1 standard deviation. * For Scharenberg et al. (2023), a batch size of 32 was used due to its smaller pool of 1061 relevant genes. Schmidt1 refers to the screen measuring Interferon-γ (IFNG) and Schmidt2 measures Interleukin-2 (IL-2) following perturbation(Schmidt et al., 2022). † CAR-T refers to an unpublished dataset.Selected GenesJaccard Similarity Between\n\t\t\tgenerated by the authors of this paper\n\t\t\tGene Search TermsSolution: 1. IPO9 2. MDM2 3. TERF1 4. DDX31 5. WDR89 6. DDX21 7. DCLRE1B 8. NOLC1 9. USP7 10. FERMT2 Language Model Language Model","level":1,"path_id":"1~26","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":31,"section_id":"sec-1~26"}],"citations":[{"raw_text":"Introducing claude\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAnthropic\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2023","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Anthropic\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2024\n\t\t\n\t\n\tIntroducing claude 3.5 sonnet","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Introducing the next generation of claude\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAnthropic\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2024","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"AutoGPT: An experimental open-source attempt to make gpt-4 fully autonomous\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAutogpt\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2023","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[{"ref_id":"b3","section_path_id":"3","quote":"(AutoGPT, 2023)","offset":883}]},{"raw_text":"Autonomous chemical research with large language models\n\t\t\n\t\t\tADaniil\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tRobertBoiko\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tBenMacknight\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tGabeKline\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tGomes\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tNature\n\t\t\n\t\t\t624\n\t\t\t7992\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t2023","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":"Autonomous chemical research with large language models","anchors":[{"ref_id":"b4","section_path_id":"1","quote":"Boiko et al., 2023;","offset":434},{"ref_id":"b4","section_path_id":"6","quote":"(Boiko et al., 2023;","offset":655}]},{"raw_text":"Rasa2 ablation in t cells boosts antigen sensitivity and long-term function\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJuliaCarnevale\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tEricShifrut\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tNupuraKale\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWilliamANyberg\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tFranziskaBlaeschke\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tYanYi Chen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tZhongmeiLi\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tPSagar\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMorganEBapat\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDiolaiti\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tO'Patrick\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tLeary\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tNature\n\t\t\n\t\t\t609\n\t\t\t7925\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\t2022","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":"Rasa2 ablation in t cells boosts antigen sensitivity and long-term function","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Genept: A simple but hard-to-beat foundation model for genes and cells built from chatgpt\n\t\t\n\t\t\tTYiqun\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJamesChen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tZou\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tbioRxiv\n\t\t\n\t\t\t2023","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":"Genept: A simple but hard-to-beat foundation model for genes and cells built from chatgpt","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Efficient generation of transcriptomic profiles by random composite measurements\n\t\t\n\t\t\tBrianCleary\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tLeCong\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAntheaCheung\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tEricSLander\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\